he same computation, or whether intervening on F_x alone with F_y left to the prior is the right way to model it.\n\nUnder A-FDT the other copy's action is held at its prior, so my choice only moves my own node. Working through the payoffs, submitting comes out ahead by a year or two whatever the other copy's probability of submitting, so A-FDT points to defecting. The one way out would be to argue the two observation sets make the functions logically identical, but the essay's own example keeps them as separate nodes, so I'll go with defecting.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing whether A-FDT treats the other copy's action as dependent on my function output. Since the copy runs the same function on different observations, I'm asking whether my node F_x and its node F_y are really the same computation, or whether intervening on F_x alone with F_y left to the prior is the right way to model it.\n\nUnder A-FDT the other copy's action is held at its prior, so my choice only moves my own node. Working through the payoffs, submitting comes out ahead by a year or two whatever the other copy's probability of submitting, so A-FDT points to defecting. The one way out would be to argue the two observation sets make the functions logically identical, but the essay's own example keeps them as separate nodes, so I'll go with defecting.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10363,"completion_tokens":1044,"total_tokens":11407,"cost":0.062332,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.062332,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.041452,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02088},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":541,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}